ever since the turn of the millennium  we've seen a massive trend towards free  to use software games and apps  everything from facebook google and  youtube to robinhood clash of clans and  spotify these services have become  masters at alternative modes of  monetization whether that be collecting  data or serving ads from a macro  perspective these free services are  awesome as they have made access to  information and content easier and  cheaper than ever before and in some  cases it makes no sense to spend money  on these services for example if you  watch a random youtube video here and  there then it probably doesn't make  sense to buy youtube premium similarly  in some cases there may be some pride in  not spending any money maybe you want to  max out your clash of clans account  without gems or maybe you want to be a  default god on fortnite putting such  scenarios aside though if you spend any  amount of substantial time on these free  to use services i'd argue that the  sacrifices that you're making on privacy  time and the opportunity to be a  customer are far greater than any  monetary cost that you could have paid  instead and here's why  starting with the most obvious sacrifice  we're making with free services we have  the sacrifice of privacy nowadays user  data is one of the most valuable forms  of currency in the world and this data  can be exchanged for billions of dollars  we all know that big tech and especially  google collects loads of data on all of  us but have you ever looked into what  exactly this data is well security.org  did and here's what they found starting  with the reasonable data we have name  phone number payment information and  email address these are all basic data  that google would need to distinguish  between users and appropriately charge  each user for subscriptions that they  may have this is likely most of the data  they need to provide users with a great  experience but this is just the  beginning of the data collection moving  on google collects data on emails you  write and receive your stored videos and  photos your stored documents your stored  spreadsheets and your youtube comments  so basically everything you store with  them and everything you communicate  through them google has advanced machine  learning and ai algorithms that can comb  through your photos and emails which  allows them to understand your lifestyle  who you spend your time with and your  hobbies for example you might have a ton  of pictures of your dog in this case you  would be a prime target for dog food and  dog toy ads similarly maybe you have a  bunch of pictures at the beach this  would make you a prime target for  flights to beaches and luxury beachside  resorts anyway next up google also  collects in-detail data regarding how  exactly you're accessing their services  we've got ip address system activity  data about interactions between apps  browser type device type application  version number carrier name and  operating system in terms of location  we've got gps data sensor data and even  information about things near the device  you're using and i haven't even covered  this activity section which has another  dozen data points about your calls and  interaction history clearly google  collects data on basically all facets of  your life and this is the same case with  facebook and twitter but what does this  even lead to  well the answer is low roi for you and  google  advertisers aren't stupid they're not  gonna run marketing campaigns that  aren't profitable sure a startup might  do this to generate a base of users but  do you really think that apple or nvidia  or amd is gonna run unprofitable ads  no and given all the data google has  collected for them their marketing  campaigns are more effective than ever  before  this means that the average person will  end up spending way more money than they  ever need to and i'm not talking about  valuable services there's definitely  going to be people who get asked for  things like audible and skillshare and  they're going to get a subscription and  they're going to get a bunch of value  from the service  rather i'm talking about consumerism  buying a new phone that you don't need  or going on a vacation that you can't  afford now again if you're doing well  for yourself and making 200 or 300 000  per year this doesn't matter for you you  can afford to splurge on a new phone  every year and go on vacation regularly  but for the average american making 50  or 60 000 per year these are expenses  that simply don't need to be in their  lives it would be much smarter for them  to save and invest this money into a  house or an index fund and that might  have been what they would have done if  it wasn't for such effective marketing  now i'm not faulting companies for using  such advertising mediums as that simply  a brilliant business decision but from  the consumer side sure you didn't have  to spend money to use google however  your net roi is likely thousands of  dollars in the red  at the same time it's not like google is  making much money off of you  individually either last year google  pulled in a total of 210 billion dollars  from advertising this includes google  search youtube and all other sources of  google related advertising now that is  an absurd amount of revenue but you have  to consider that google also has an  absurd amount of customers literally  over half the world uses google with  their worldwide user account coming in  at 4.3 billion if we divide their  advertising revenue by their user  account we get just 48.72  per customer and that's revenue google's  net income margin sits at just under 30  percent meaning that less than 15 of  that is actually profit now the real  numbers are likely much more skewed  there are likely billions of users that  only profit google one or two dollars  per year and there's likely billions who  profit them 50 or 60 per year but i  think you get the point google isn't  making hundreds or thousands of dollars  from each customer but by collecting  data a lot of their users end up  spending thousands of dollars that they  don't need to be spending so wouldn't it  be a lot better if we cut out  advertisers and directly paid google  what if we paid google 20 per month for  all of their services that's 240 per  year but in return we get an ad free  data collection free private experience  across all google services  not only would that provide a far  superior user experience but it would  save the average person thousands of  dollars in unnecessary spending in the  meantime since google doesn't have to  spend money on appealing to or  coordinating advertisers they can enjoy  substantially more revenue and higher  profit margins it's a win-win scenario  and if you're not convinced by potential  financial savings or better user  experience or more privacy i think you  should strongly consider the savings in  time  while all the benefits we've discussed  so far are definitely valuable the deal  breaker for me personally is definitely  time most people rarely consider time as  a currency which is a shame given that  it's the most valuable currency we have  and when we use free services the  currency that we're often paying with is  time again this isn't that important if  you barely use free services for example  if you only spend 20 minutes on youtube  every day it's not a big deal but i know  a lot of us spend six seven nine hours  on youtube every single day myself  included if we're not working or  socializing we're probably watching  youtube and nowadays the standard on  youtube has become two five second  non-skippable ads roughly every three  minutes  this means that for every hour you spend  on youtube you're consuming 45 second  ads or 200 seconds worth of ads if you  watch three hours worth of youtube on a  daily basis you're consuming 600 seconds  worth of ads every day and 18 000  seconds worth of ads every month that's  the same as 5 hours worth of ads every  single month youtube premium's  individual plan costs 12 per month this  means that by choosing to watch ads  instead of paying for youtube premium  you're effectively selling your time for  two dollars and 40 cents per hour and  that's if you're being stupid with your  subscription the smarter play is to  obviously get the family plan and split  it with your friends family and or  roommates the family plan costs 18 per  month and allows you to add up to five  members onto the plan this means that  each member is effectively paying just  three dollars per month considering this  by choosing to watch ads you're  effectively selling your time for 60  cents per hour even if you're just  watching one hour worth of youtube per  day you're selling your time for one  dollar and 80 cents per hour if you're  watching six hours per day you're  looking at 30 cents per hour and if  you're watching nine hours per day  you're looking at just 20 cents per hour  i don't think i need to explain why  these are unfavorable trade-offs and  similar arguments can be made if you  spend your time scrolling through tock  or instagram instead by using these free  services you're not only selling your  time for laughable amounts of money but  you're spending hours every month  watching ads instead of watching the  content itself  if you thought sacrificing your privacy  your user experience and your time were  not already bad enough you're also  sacrificing your position as a customer  for example take a look at these actions  a few years ago youtube moved away from  edgy content last year robin hood halted  trading on gamestop and just a few  months ago youtube removed the dislike  button all of these actions were  strongly disapproved of by users and  made users feel like these companies  weren't listening to their customers but  in reality all of these companies were  listening to their customers and swiftly  taking action the problem is that you're  not the customer with youtube  advertisers are the customer and they  wanted less edgy content and with  robinhood the market makers were the  customers and they weren't fans of  gamestop in both of these cases even  though you're a user you're actually a  product being sold now imagine if  everyone watching youtube had a youtube  premium subscription do you think  youtube would have removed the dislike  button or moved away from edgy content  no similarly if revenue customers paid a  commission on the trades like with the  traditional brokerage do you think  gamestyle would have been halted  probably not but by choosing to be the  product we give up all the power we  could have as a customer now look at the  flip side of the situation at companies  with extremely favorable reputations  probably one of the most admired  companies in the world is apple apple  does have their fair share of flaws when  it comes to overpricing and anti-repair  but apple receives virtually no  complaints regarding the quality of  their products or the quality of their  services or the user experience or the  security of the devices or the privacy  that apple provides and this is because  with apple you're truly the customer the  same cannot be said about free to use  services  at the end of the day many companies  have flocked over to free to use models  because it gives them an extraordinary  reach this is truly awesome because it  has made great services accessible to  people all around the world and i  strongly believe that their net impact  on the world is way more positive than  negative with that being said though  there is no such thing as a free service  if you're not paying with cash you're  paying with data and a time which is  often times much more expensive so i  think companies should offer more paid  options that allow users to become  customers not only would this be more  profitable for companies but it would  allow them to earn a much better  reputation do you really think that  google wants to be infamous for  collecting data when sergey brin and  larry page founded google their slogan  was literally don't be evil so i  strongly believe that every company  would prefer to directly deal with their  users the problem is that there isn't  enough interest from us for companies to  take this option more seriously so in  the end the choice comes down to us do  we want to remain a product or would we  rather become the customer where do you  guys stand on the spectrum comment that  down below also drop a like if this  video made you look at the situation  with big tech differently and of course  consider checking out our international  channels to watch the videos in other  languages and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one  you

